! Silicon, two atoms, PAW, on the *unreduced* 2x2x2 k-grid.
!
! The same physics as si2-paw and a deliberately redundant way to sample it: all
! eight points of the shifted grid, where two would do. That is the point. With
! the full star of every k-point present, the projector occupations ``becsum``
! come out symmetric on their own, so QE's ``PAW_symmetrize`` is the identity
! and the one-centre terms can be validated without it. si2-paw, on the reduced
! set, is then the case that tests the symmetrisation and nothing else.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=2, celldm(1)=10.20, nat=2, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=20.0, ecutrho=160.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-n-kjpaw_psl.0.1.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS crystal
  8
   0.25 0.25 0.25  1.0
   0.25 0.25 0.75  1.0
   0.25 0.75 0.25  1.0
   0.25 0.75 0.75  1.0
   0.75 0.25 0.25  1.0
   0.75 0.25 0.75  1.0
   0.75 0.75 0.25  1.0
   0.75 0.75 0.75  1.0
